OWNER-RULING-2026-09-03-02. EXPAND step: v3 is created ALONGSIDE v2. No SQL
apply runs from this PR; nothing until the Auditor authorises it, staging first.

Objects reserved: get_top_contributors_v3 (created). contributor_points_since
read-only, called unchanged, grants untouched. get_top_contributors_v2 not
altered and NOT dropped — it is the rollback. No tables, policies, publication
entries, settings or indexes altered.

WHY A NEW FUNCTION. Postgres refuses to change a return type through CREATE OR
REPLACE (42P13). Verified on production: v2 returns TABLE(user_id uuid,
rank_position integer, contributor_score integer). Adding recent_score changes
that type, so v2 cannot be edited and must not be.

THE 2026-08-11 INSTRUCTION IS MARKED SUPERSEDED, NOT DELETED. The v2 migration
keeps its original text ("The 30-day number is NEVER returned") byte for byte;
the v3 file names the ruling that supersedes it and quotes it. C-46 and C-51
both came from a ruling that lived only in a chat. This one lives in the tree.

RANKING PROVED UNCHANGED, production read-only, 2026-09-03 08:45:05Z:
  pos 1  01c5059c…114a  lifetime 9551   recent 7055
  pos 2  c2f9619d…0245  lifetime 8888   recent 6978
  pos 3  cc691988…03be  lifetime 11546  recent 6823
Same three user_ids, same order, same three lifetime values from both
functions. Position 3 carries the largest lifetime number — which is the
ruling's own justification, visible in the data.

CROSS-MEMBER TEST, and it was shown failing first. Five assertions, each
demonstrated catching its own planted defect on a scratch PostgreSQL 16 built
to production's posture:
  A1 row cap        — cap removed  -> 44 rows, 41 members leaked. CAUGHT.
  A2 no personalisation — caller's own row hoisted. CAUGHT.
  A3 helper stays shut  — helper granted to anon. CAUGHT.
  A4 frozen signature   — posts_count added. CAUGHT.
  A5 posture / v2 alive — v2 renamed away. CAUGHT.
All five pass against the real v3 this migration creates. The probe runs
BEGIN…ROLLBACK and writes nothing.

GRANTS MATCHED, NOT EXCEEDED. v2's real ACL on production is
{=X/postgres,postgres=X,anon=X,authenticated=X,service_role=X} — the leading
=X is PUBLIC, which v2's own migration comment does not mention. v3 revokes
PUBLIC and grants anon + authenticated: a subset of v2's reach, never a
superset. Two findings raised, neither fixed here:
  * comment and catalogue disagree on v2's grants (Standing Rule 21);
  * REVOKE EXECUTE ... FROM anon on v2 does NOT stop anon executing it while
    PUBLIC holds the grant — reproduced on the scratch instance. The obvious
    command for taking v2 out of service silently does not work.

ROLLBACK exercised: refuses and changes nothing when v2 is missing, and when
v2 exists but anon cannot reach it; drops cleanly otherwise; idempotent on a
second run. Client-first ordering is written into its header.

BLOCKING FINDING: docs/gates/TC-v3-interface.md exists on NO ref. Every remote
branch and tag scanned; docs/gates/ holds only GATE_REGISTER.md and
phase-0-kickoff.md. Until the Auditor commits it, this migration is the only
place the frozen shape is written down, by the party the freeze constrains.
Recorded, not worked around. docs/gates/** is not mine to author.

